{"timestamp": "2026-07-25T14:28:27.517975", "data": {"url": "https://computerhistory.org/apple-timeline/", "title": "Apple Timeline - CHM", "content": "Home Apple@50 Timeline Share Fifty Years of Innovation Apple Inc. is one of the world\u2019s most valuable companies. It revolutionized computing over and over with products and services ranging from the Apple II and the Macintosh to iTunes and the iPhone. Founded in a humble garage by Steve Jobs and Steve Wozniak, Apple has undergone good times and bad, including over a decade without Jobs, who returned to orchestrate perhaps the most successful corporate turnaround in history. Easy to use and beautifully designed, Apple\u2019s products embody and promote both creativity and iconoclasm, inspiring passionate loyalty from users and fans. Follow Apple through key moments in the company\u2019s 50-year history to date. Explore Apple History 1971 Steve Wozniak meets Steve Jobs Bill Fernandez, a mutual friend of both Wozniak and Jobs, introduces the two. With a shared interest in electronics, the two Steves hit it off. Image credit: Steve Jobs (left) and Steve Wozniak (right) high school yearbook photos. Homestead High School, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. March 5, 1975 The Homebrew Computer Club begins to meet Steve Wozniak attends the first Homebrew Computer Club meeting. He will start showing off his new Apple I board design at future meetings, held in the auditorium of the Stanford Linear Accelerator Center (SLAC), shown in the image above. Images: (main) Gift of Lee Felsenstein, 102702618; (inset) Steve Wozniak (standing) and Steve Jobs (seated). Courtesy of Joe Melena, 102665181. December 8, 1975 The Byte Shop opens Paul Terrell opens the first Byte Shop store in Mountain View. Terrell\u2019s order of 50 assembled Apple I boards motivates the Steves to start a company. Image: Courtesy of Paul Terrell. April 1, 1976 Apple Computer Company is founded Apple Computer Company is founded as a partnership between Jobs, Woz, and Ron Wayne. Wayne pulls out eleven days later. View the Apple-1 Operation Manual here . Image: Computer History Museum, 102646518. January 3, 1977 Apple Computer incorporates In late 1976, Mike Markkula meets the Steves, agrees to invest in the company and ends up writing the business plan. Apple incorporates as Apple Computer, Inc. the following January. Markkula meets Jobs Writing the business plan Image: Gift of Mike Markkula, 102789075. April 16, 1977 Apple II debuts Woz and Jobs show off the Apple II at the first West Coast Computer Faire. With color graphics and eight expansion slots, it\u2019s a hit. Image: Photo by Roger Ressmeyer/Corbis/VCG via Getty Images. December 1979 Jobs visits Xerox PARC and is inspired by the Smalltalk GUI Steve Jobs and his Apple team visit Xerox PARC, seeing the graphical user interface for the first time. Jobs decides the Lisa Computer project must incorporate the GUI. Images: (main) Smalltalk-78 emulated in the Smalltalk Zoo , showing the demo given to Steve Jobs by Dan Ingalls in 1979. Screenshot by CHM Curator Hansen Hsu; (inset) Alto at Xerox PARC. Courtesy of the PARC Library, 102710356. December 12, 1980 Apple goes public Apple goes public at $22 a share, generating over $100 million underwritten by Morgan-Stanley and boutique investment bank Hambrecht & Quist. Jobs and Woz are instant millionaires. View the Preliminary Confidential Offering Memorandum and Stock Options here . Image: Gift of Mike Markkula, 102783503. January 19, 1983 Apple introduces the Lisa Apple introduces the Lisa, the first consumer PC with a graphical user interface and a mouse. At a pricey $9,995, Lisa fails in the marketplace. Image: Computer History Museum, 102634506. January 24, 1984 Macintosh brings graphical computing to more users Jobs unveils the Macintosh along with the famous \u201c1984\u201d ad. At a quarter of the cost of Lisa, it brings the GUI to \u201cthe rest of us.\u201d Image: Courtesy of Rick English, 102808274. September 17, 1985 Steve Jobs resigns from Apple CEO John Sculley demotes Jobs from his position as Macintosh division head in April and thwarts an attempted boardroom coup by Jobs in May. After a summer pondering his future, Jobs leaves to start NeXT Computer in September. Images: (main) John Sculley. Photo by Ann E. Yow-Dyson/Getty Images; (inset) NeXT senior staff, including Steve Jobs, standing third from left. Photo via Getty Images / Stringer / Hulton Archive. October 21, 1991 Macintosh PowerBook introduces the modern clamshell laptop PowerBooks were the first laptops to have the keyboard back near the screen, leaving room for palm rests and a trackball (later trackpad) at the front. Image: Computer History Museum, 102639965. Photo by CHM Curator Hansen Hsu, edited by Aurora Tucker. December 2, 1991 Apple releases QuickTime Apple\u2019s QuickTime is the first consumer-level digital video format for personal computers. Released for Macintosh OS System 6, QuickTime is later bundled with System 7 Pro. Image: Gift of Mark Richards, 102798417. August 2, 1993 Newton MessagePad ships Newton MessagePad, Apple\u2019s first handheld computing device, ships. Its troubled launch contributes to John Sculley\u2019s replacement by Michael Spindler as CEO. Image: Newton MessagePad , 1993. Collection of the Computer History Museum, X2358.2002A. Photo \u00a9Mark Richards. March 14, 1994 Macintosh transitions to PowerPC processors Apple introduces the first Macintosh computers that use PowerPC RISC chips, the result of a collaboration with IBM and Motorola. The PowerPC chips replace the Motorola 68000 series chips used in earlier Macs. Image: Gift of Thomas H. Bennett, 102711296. 1997 Steve Jobs returns Needing a new OS, Apple acquires Jobs\u2019 NeXT in February. In June, Jobs replaces Gil Amelio as CEO. He kills off most of the existing product line, including Newton, and simplifies Apple\u2019s offerings to just four Macs. Image: Gil Amelio speaking at Macworld Expo, January 1997, where he announced the acquisition of NeXT. Photo by David Butow/Corbis via Getty Images. March 24, 2001 Apple releases Mac OS X 10.0 Cheetah Mac OS X, based on the NeXTSTEP OS, becomes the foundation of all future Apple operating systems, replacing the classic Mac OS. Image: Gift of Kenneth Russell, 102747741. 2001 Apple revolutionizes music with iTunes and iPod In January, iTunes debuts, allowing users to play MP3s and burn them to CD. In October, Apple releases the first iPod MP3 player. Two years later, the company opens the iTunes Music Store, selling songs at 99 cents and transforming the music industry. Images: (main) Computer History Museum, Gift of Jon Rubinstein, 102741160; (inset) Apple\u2019s 2001 \u201cRip. Mix. Burn.\u201d ad featured iTunes. Credit \u00a9 Apple, Inc. January 7, 2003 Apple launches its own web browser, Safari Safari, based on an open-source engine, ends Apple\u2019s reliance on Microsoft\u2019s Internet Explorer browser. Safari will later become crucial for the success of the iPhone. Image: Photo by John G. Mabanglo /AFP via Getty Images. June 2005 Apple announces transition to Intel processors With Motorola and IBM falling behind in performance and energy efficiency, Apple announces it is transitioning the chip architecture in the Mac again, this time from PowerPC to Intel x86. The first Intel Macs arrive in January of the next year. Image: Photo by David Paul Morris/Getty Images. June 29, 2007 iPhone reinvents the mobile phone The iPhone revolutionizes smartphone design with its multitouch interface. The Apple App Store opens the following year. Third party apps transform the iPhone into a multipurpose device \u201cin your pocket.\u201d Image: Photo by David Paul Morris/Getty Images. April 3. 2010 iPad Arrives Jobs introduces the iPad tablet computer, using Apple\u2019s first custom designed ARM chip, the A4. It uses the same OS and App Store as the iPhone. Image: Photo by Simon Lees/PC Plus Magazine via Getty Images. October 4, 2011 The iPhone 4S launches with Siri Siri initially showed promise, letting users control functions of the iPhone with their voice and have funny conversations. But it failed to keep up with competing voice assistants like Amazon\u2019s Alexa. Image: LPETTET via Getty Images. October 5, 2011 Steve Jobs passes away and Tim Cook becomes CEO After a long battle with pancreatic cancer, Steve Jobs passes away. COO Tim Cook takes over as CEO. Image: Photo by Kevork Djansezian/Getty Image. April 24, 2015 Apple Watch combines technology, fashion, and fitness Cook unveils the Apple Watch. Designed by Jony Ive as a high fashion accessory, sales start slow but pick up after pivoting to a focus on fitness. Image: Photo by Stephen Lam/Getty Images. December 2016 AirPods offers seamless wireless connection Apple releases AirPods wireless earbuds for iPhones, Apple Watches, and other devices. Today, AirPods are Apple\u2019s most popular wearable computing device. Image: Photo by Stephen Lam/Getty Images. August 2, 2018 Apple becomes the first company to reach a market value of $1 trillion Under Tim Cook\u2019s leadership, Apple\u2019s market valuation breaks $1 trillion, the first for any company ever. It breaks further records when it reaches $2 trillion in 2020 and $3 trillion in 2022. Image: Photo by Filip Radwanski/SOPA Images/LightRocket via Getty Images. 2020 Mac transitions to Apple Silicon Building on its iPhone and iPad chip design expertise, in June, Apple announces its third Mac CPU transition, from Intel to custom-designed \u201cApple Silicon\u201d ARM chips. The first Macs with the new M1 chips arrive in November, dramatically increasing performance and battery life. Image: Henriok , CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. 2024 Apple\u2019s present and future In February, Apple releases its first mixed-reality headset, the Vision Pro, at $3,500. In June, the company announces Apple Intelligence, its AI strategy. Both receive mixed reviews. How will Apple predict and respond to the next generation of cutting-edge tech? Image: Photo by Julien de Rosa/AFP via Getty Images. Share HELP US TELL THE FULL STORY Become part of the movement transforming how people understand our computing history, digital present, and future impact on humanity by donating today. Donate", "lists": [["Explore Overview Collections Timelines Blogs Stories Playlists Podcast Activities & Resources", "Overview", "Collections", "Timelines", "Blogs", "Stories", "Playlists", "Podcast", "Activities & Resources", "Visit Overview Plan Your Visit Exhibits Tours Group Visits Events Venue Shop", "Overview", "Plan Your Visit", "Exhibits", "Tours", "Group Visits", "Events", "Venue", "Shop", "About Overview This Is CHM Awards Programs News Leadership", "Overview", "This Is CHM", "Awards", "Programs", "News", "Leadership", "Join & Give Overview Membership Ways to Give Donor Recognition Institutional Supporters Volunteers", "Overview", "Membership", "Ways to Give", "Donor Recognition", "Institutional Supporters", "Volunteers", "", "Search", "Donate", "Tickets"], ["Overview", "Collections", "Timelines", "Blogs", "Stories", "Playlists", "Podcast", "Activities & Resources"], ["Overview", "Plan Your Visit", "Exhibits", "Tours", "Group Visits", "Events", "Venue", "Shop"], ["Overview", "This Is CHM", "Awards", "Programs", "News", "Leadership"], ["Overview", "Membership", "Ways to Give", "Donor Recognition", "Institutional Supporters", "Volunteers"], ["Hours & Admission", "Subscribe", "Account"], ["CHM is a registered 501(c)3 organization. Your donation is tax-deductible to the extent permitted by law.", "Tax ID 77-0507525.", "Terms of Use", "Privacy", "\u00a9 2025 Computer History Museum"], ["Press", "Careers", "Venue"], ["Events", "Exhibits", "Donate"], ["Medium", "Facebook", "X", "Youtube", "Instagram", "TikTok", "Linkedin"]], "tables": [], "code_blocks": [], "meta_description": "", "meta_keywords": "", "og_image": "https://computerhistory.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Macintosh-by-Rick-English-102808274-05-006-src-scaled.jpg", "js_rendered": true, "js_message": "Page appears to be rendered by a JavaScript framework; content may be incomplete.", "success": true, "error": "", "truncated": false, "fetched_bytes": 113176, "total_bytes": 113176}}